    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d43-a6">

                <pb facs="0420" n="408" xml:id="pg408"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Enclosure]</seg></opener>
                <head>I</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-06-06">6 <hi rendition="#small-caps">June</hi>,
                        1919.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#italic">Copy of Letter From the President of
                        the Labour Committee to the President of the Peace Conference</hi></p>
                <p>In two sittings held at the Labour Ministry on the 3rd and 4th June, 1919, the
                    Labour Committee set up in accordance with the decisions of the Supreme Council
                    of the 30th April<anchor n="14" xml:id="d43-f1"/> and 10th May, 1919,<anchor
                        n="15" xml:id="d43-f2"/> examined, so far as they relate to the labour
                    question, the remarks of the German Delegates on the peace conditions.<anchor
                        n="16" xml:id="d43-f3"/></p>
                <p>The majority of the observations put forward by the German Delegation were
                    already included in the two notes previously submitted by that Delegation on the
                        10th<anchor n="17" xml:id="d43-f4"/> and 22nd May, 1919,<anchor n="18"
                        xml:id="d43-f5"/> and to which replies were sent<anchor n="19"
                        xml:id="d43-f6"/> in accordance with the proposals of the Committee dated
                    13th and 26th May. The Committee did not consequently think it desirable to
                    resume the examination of the questions already dealt with in these notes and in
                    the replies which have been made to them.</p>
                <p>The only two points on which it thought it useful to present a reply are the
                    following:— <list>
                        <label>(1)</label>
                        <item>The admission of Germany to the League of Nations as a corollary to
                            her admission to the international labour organization.</item>
                        <label>(2)</label>
                        <item>The offer of Germany to supply labour for the restoration of the
                            devastated regions.</item>
                    </list></p>
                <p>At its meeting of the 4th June, 1919, the Labour Committee adopted on those two
                    points the resolutions, copy of which I have the honour to submit to you
                    herewith.</p>
                <p>There were present at this meeting:— <table cols="2" rend="width: 70%;"
                        rendition="#center" rows="6">
                        <row role="label">
                            <cell>For the United States of America</cell>
                            <cell>Mr. J. T. Shotwell</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>For the British Empire</cell>
                            <cell>Mr. G. N. Barnes.</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell rows="2">For France</cell>
                            <cell>Mr. Colliard.</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>Mr. Arthur Fontaine.</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>For Italy</cell>
                            <cell>Mr. di Palma Castiglione</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>For Japan</cell>
                            <cell>Mr. Otchiai.</cell>
                        </row>
                    </table></p>
                <pb facs="0421" n="409" xml:id="pg409"/>
                <p>There were also present at the meeting the following two representatives of
                    Belgium:—</p>
                <p rendition="#center">Messrs. Anseele and Coppieters.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">G. N. Barnes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
